              "procedure": "Test 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) for every personal-property asset and include it independently alongside any applicable specific exemption, including actual wearing apparel under (a), a motor vehicle under (c), or a professionally prescribed health aid under (e).",
              "avoid_when": "Do not omit (b) merely because a specific subsection applies; do not use the personal-property exemptions for business-use property, classify jewelry as wearing apparel, or classify an ordinary household appliance as a tool of trade without facts showing trade use."
            },
            {
              "tasks": [
                "EV"
              ],
              "when": "Illinois exemptions are allowable and claim values must be reported independently.",
              "procedure": "For every personal-use asset, first report § 5/12-1001(b) at the lesser of value and $4,000 for one debtor or $8,000 for two joint debtors; this alternative remains required when a specific subsection also applies. Then separately add a motor vehicle under (c) capped at $2,400, or actual clothing under (a) and a professionally prescribed health aid under (e) at the asset value because the supplied text states no dollar cap.",
              "avoid_when": "Do not substitute the (b) amount for the $2,400 motor-vehicle limit, double the motor-vehicle limit, omit (b) beside clothing or a health aid, apply (a) to jewelry or watches, or apply the trade-tool provision without facts showing trade use."
            }
          ],
          "operators": [],
          "jurisdiction": "ILLINOIS"
        }
      },
      "source_case_ids": [
        219,
        221,
        224
      ],
      "rationale": "In source 224 EC, the parent correctly included § 5/12-1001(b) but falsely added wearing-apparel subsection (a) to a bracelet. Sources 219 and 221 distinguish actual clothing, which receives (a) plus (b), from necklaces and bracelets, which receive only (b). The edit adds only that factual exception and preserves the committed EV guidance verbatim."
